# Bars: geom_bar

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_bar()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_bar.html), which covers `geom_col()`
and `stat_count()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

There are two kinds of bar chart. `Giggly.Plot.geom_bar/3` counts: each bar's height is the number
of rows at its `x`, or the sum of their weights. `Giggly.Plot.geom_col/3` draws heights that are
already in the data: one bar per row, from 0 to its `y`. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and
drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`,
so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the
cell with `Kino.Image`. Most examples use `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for
234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's name
for it. The rest make small tables of their own, as ggplot2's do.

## Counting the cars in each class

```r
# geom_bar is designed to make it easy to create bar charts that show
# counts (or sums of weights)
g <- ggplot(mpg, aes(class))
# Number of cars in each class:
g + geom_bar()
```

The counting goes into `y`, and the y axis is titled `count`.

```elixir
plot = Plot.new(mpg, %{x: :class})

plot
|> Plot.geom_bar()
|> render.()
```

## Summing a weight

```r
# Total engine displacement of each class
g + geom_bar(aes(weight = displ))
```

Mapping `weight` makes each bar the sum of its rows' weights rather than their number. The y axis
is still titled `count`, as ggplot2's is.

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.geom_bar(%{weight: :displ})
|> render.()
```

## Horizontal bars

```r
# Map class to y instead to flip the orientation
ggplot(mpg) + geom_bar(aes(y = class))
```

Counting goes along whichever of `x` and `y` is mapped, so mapping `y` draws the bars rightward
and titles the x axis `count`.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new()
|> Plot.geom_bar(%{y: :class})
|> render.()
```

## Stacked bars

```r
# Bar charts are automatically stacked when multiple bars are placed
# at the same location. The order of the fill is designed to match
# the legend
g + geom_bar(aes(fill = drv))
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `fill`, the scale that maps it and the legend that reads it back.
Bars at the same `x` already stack, one for each `group`, but drawn in one grey without borders,
a stack looks like a single bar.

## Reversing the stack

```r
# If you need to flip the order (because you've flipped the orientation)
# call position_stack() explicitly:
ggplot(mpg, aes(y = class)) +
 geom_bar(aes(fill = drv), position = position_stack(reverse = TRUE)) +
 theme(legend.position = "top")
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `fill`, `position_stack()` and its `reverse` option, and changing the
theme, here to put the legend above the plot. Giggly names a position by an atom, `position:
:stack`, which takes no options yet.

## Columns

```r
# To show (e.g.) means, you need geom_col()
df <- data.frame(trt = c("a", "b", "c"), outcome = c(2.3, 1.9, 3.2))
ggplot(df, aes(trt, outcome)) +
  geom_col()
```

A table can be a keyword list of columns, as R's `data.frame()` is written.

```elixir
outcomes = [trt: ["a", "b", "c"], outcome: [2.3, 1.9, 3.2]]

outcomes
|> Plot.new(%{x: :trt, y: :outcome})
|> Plot.geom_col()
|> render.()
```

## The same data as points

```r
# But geom_point() displays exactly the same information and doesn't
# require the y-axis to touch zero.
ggplot(df, aes(trt, outcome)) +
  geom_point()
```

```elixir
outcomes
|> Plot.new(%{x: :trt, y: :outcome})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

## Counting continuous values

```r
# You can also use geom_bar() with continuous data, in which case
# it will show counts at unique locations
df <- data.frame(x = rep(c(2.9, 3.1, 4.5), c(5, 10, 4)))
ggplot(df, aes(x)) + geom_bar()
```

R's `rep()` repeats each value as many times as its count says. Each bar is 0.9 as wide as the
closest two values are apart, here 2.9 and 3.1, so the bars are narrow.

```elixir
values = List.duplicate(2.9, 5) ++ List.duplicate(3.1, 10) ++ List.duplicate(4.5, 4)

[x: values]
|> Plot.new(%{x: :x})
|> Plot.geom_bar()
|> render.()
```

## A histogram of the same data

```r
# cf. a histogram of the same data
ggplot(df, aes(x)) + geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.5)
```

Not in Giggly yet: histograms, `geom_histogram()`, and the binning they count with,
`stat_bin()`.

## Aligning columns with dates

```r
# Use `just` to control how columns are aligned with axis breaks:
df <- data.frame(x = as.Date(c("2020-01-01", "2020-02-01")), y = 1:2)
# Columns centered on the first day of the month
ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) + geom_col(just = 0.5)
# Columns begin on the first day of the month
ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) + geom_col(just = 1)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_col()`'s `just` option. Giggly centres every bar
on its `x`, as `just = 0.5` does.
